  <h1>Bin Store Prices: How the Weekly Price Drop Works</h1>
  <p class="lead">A bin store does not price items one by one. It charges one flat price for anything in the bins, and that price steps down day by day through the week. This page explains the pricing cycle in detail: what restock day does to the price, why the steps exist, what happens at the end of the week, and why no directory, including this one, can honestly print a bin store's prices. It describes the model in general; each store sets its own numbers.</p>

  <h2>One price for everything in the bin</h2>
  <p>The starting point is that bin store goods arrive unpriced. A store buys mixed pallets of returned and overstock goods, and nobody at the store checks, tests, or labels each item. Pricing items one at a time, the way an ordinary shop does, would cost more in labor than many of the items are worth. So the store skips it. Everything in the bins costs the same flat amount per item, whatever it is: a phone case, a toy, a kitchen gadget, or something that originally sold for far more. The flat price is not a discount on each item's value. It is an average, charged blind, across goods the store itself has not sorted.</p>
  <p>That blind average is why the model can produce both bargains and duds in the same bin. An item worth much more than the flat price and an item worth nothing sit side by side at the same price. The store does not know which is which, and mostly does not try to find out. Shoppers who understand this treat the flat price as the cost of the hunt, not as a valuation of what they pull out.</p>

  <h2>Restock day sets the top price</h2>
  <p>Most bin stores run a weekly cycle that starts on restock day, when the bins are emptied of leftovers and refilled with a fresh load. On that day the flat price is at its highest for the week, because the stock is freshest and the choice is widest. Stores commonly advertise restock day heavily, and it is usually the busiest day of the week, with lines forming before opening at popular locations.</p>
  <p>The higher opening price is not arbitrary. Fresh stock contains the week's best items, and they will be picked out quickly. The store prices the first day for the shoppers who want first pick, in the same way that any seller prices scarce goods higher while they are plentiful and desirable. What the store cannot do is hold that price: every day the bins sit, the best goods leave and the average quality of what remains falls.</p>

  <h2>The daily steps down</h2>
  <p>After restock day, the flat price drops in steps, usually daily. A store might open the week at one price, knock a fixed amount off each following day, and end the cycle at a small fraction of the opening price. The steps exist because the stock is decaying in value: not going stale like food, but being picked over, so each day's bins hold a worse mix than the day before. Falling prices match falling average quality, and they keep shoppers coming back through the week instead of only on restock day.</p>
  <p>The steps also clear the floor. A bin store's economics depend on turning the whole load over before the next one arrives, because storage space is the scarcest thing the store has. Whatever is left at the end of the week is the stock nobody wanted at any earlier price, and the final low price is the store's way of converting it to cash and empty bins instead of paying to haul it away.</p>

  <h2>The end of the week: final prices and fill-a-bag days</h2>
  <p>Many stores close the cycle with an extra mechanism on the last day or two. Some drop to a final clearance price per item. Some run a fill-a-bag format, where a bag of a set size costs a flat amount and everything that fits in it is included. Some bundle the leftovers for resale to smaller shops. The details vary, but the purpose is the same: the floor must be empty when the next load lands.</p>
  <p>End-of-week shopping is the mirror image of restock day. The price per item is the lowest of the week, and so is the chance of finding something valuable. What remains has been looked at and passed over by every shopper who came earlier. Experienced bin store shoppers treat the two ends of the week as different activities: restock day is a search for quality, the last day is a search for bulk and luck.</p>

  <h2>Why two bin stores charge different prices</h2>
  <p>Shoppers sometimes ask why one store opens its week higher than another store across town. The main reasons are costs and loads. Stores pay different prices for pallets depending on the source and quality of the load, they pay different rents, and they face different local competition. A store whose loads come from a higher-end retailer's returns can charge more, because the average item is worth more. A store buying cheaper, rougher loads must charge less, because its bins hold more broken and incomplete goods. The opening price is therefore a rough signal about the load, not just a number: very cheap weeks usually mean cheaper goods, not a generous store.</p>

  <h2>Why this directory prints no prices</h2>
  <p>Bin store prices change every day by design, and stores adjust their steps and restock days without notice. A price printed on a directory page would be wrong most of the week it was read. So Bin Stores USA lists names and addresses, and links where a working public page exists, and leaves prices and schedules to each store's own page, which is the only place they are current. The <a href="/guide/">guide to how bin stores work</a> covers the wider model, and the <a href="/bin-stores-vs-outlets/">comparison of bin stores, outlets, and thrift stores</a> explains how this pricing differs from the by-the-pound and tagged-price models nearby.</p>
